WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.770
Spoiler alert!

00:00:01.770 --> 00:00:04.931
Swimming, a popular form
of exercise, easy on the joints,

00:00:04.931 --> 00:00:08.180
turns out to not be good
for weight loss.

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:10.343
You're working hard,
burning calories,

00:00:10.343 --> 00:00:12.563
so, why don't the inches
and pounds come off?

00:00:12.648 --> 00:00:15.602
Watch the video to find out.

00:00:15.602 --> 00:00:24.000
"Is Swimming Good for Weight Loss?"

00:00:26.100 --> 00:00:30.180
Swimming and aquatic exercise
in general, are popular alternatives

00:00:30.180 --> 00:00:32.880
to land-based activities
such as walking or biking.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:34.560
The buoyancy helps take some

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:36.420
of the weight-bearing stress
off of joints,

00:00:36.420 --> 00:00:41.160
but swimming appears to be
less effective for weight loss.

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:44.160
Obese women were randomized
into an hour a day

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:46.860
of walking, cycling, or swimming.

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:50.580
Six months later the walkers
lost an average of 17 pounds,

00:00:50.580 --> 00:00:53.700
the cyclists lost
an average of 19 pounds,

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:57.000
and the swimmers
didn’t lose an ounce

00:00:57.000 --> 00:00:59.640
(in fact, they actually
gained 5 pounds).

00:00:59.640 --> 00:01:01.980
Gauging skin folds
to estimate body fat,

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:04.440
the measurements slimmed
more than 40 percent

00:01:04.440 --> 00:01:06.180
in the walking
and cycling groups,

00:01:06.180 --> 00:01:09.000
but there was no change at all
in the swimming group.

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:11.820
What’s going on?
And check this out.

00:01:11.820 --> 00:01:14.220
The more the women walked,
the more they lost weight.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:17.100
The more the women biked,
the more they lost weight.

00:01:17.100 --> 00:01:20.340
But the more laps they did
didn’t seem to matter.

00:01:20.340 --> 00:01:22.980
Even an hour a day.
No weight loss.

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:25.920
What is going on?

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:28.320
Well, it turns out
that some exercise

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:30.660
boosts appetite
more than others.

00:01:30.660 --> 00:01:33.420
While land-based exercise
does not stimulate

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:36.240
a compensatory increase
in appetite and calorie intake,

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:39.480
the same cannot be said
for water-based exercise.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:45.900
In contrast to walking,
in contrast to running,

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:49.320
and in contrast
to cycling,

00:01:49.320 --> 00:01:53.580
swimming can significantly
heighten hunger within hours.

00:01:53.580 --> 00:01:56.700
This may explain why swimmers
tend to have more body fat

00:01:56.700 --> 00:01:59.340
than runners of equal caliber,
even though they may be

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:02.040
actually expending more
calories during training.

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:04.680
If anything, you’d think
swimming might lead

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:07.860
to even greater weight loss since
you’re losing heat to the water.

00:02:07.860 --> 00:02:10.680
But swimming didn’t
seem to work at all.

00:02:11.280 --> 00:02:16.020
The cold, it turns out,
may actually be the culprit.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:20.160
If you exercise
in warm water (about 90o F)

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:23.220
it does not boost your appetite
more than exercising on land.

00:02:23.220 --> 00:02:26.520
After the same workout
in cool water (about 70° F),

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:29.460
people can end up eating
more than twice as much

00:02:29.460 --> 00:02:30.480
at a meal an hour later.

00:02:30.480 --> 00:02:34.380
Maybe they’re just burning off
extra calories to stay warm?

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:37.680
No, even at the same number
of calories expended,

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:42.360
people eat hundreds of calories
more after exercising in cold water.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:46.020
Offered a buffet after burning off
about 500 calories in cool water,

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:50.280
people eat nearly 900 calories,
hundreds more than after exercising

00:02:50.280 --> 00:02:52.440
in warm water
or just staying dry.

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:55.320
So, they ended up taking
in about twice as many

00:02:55.320 --> 00:02:56.820
calories as they exercised off.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:00.360
No wonder swimming doesn’t
appear useful for weight loss.

00:03:00.360 --> 00:03:02.040
Would the same
thing happen

00:03:02.040 --> 00:03:04.080
under different
temperatures on land?

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:07.320
A team of British researchers
sought to find out,

00:03:07.320 --> 00:03:09.420
randomizing people
to briskly walk

00:03:09.420 --> 00:03:13.320
for 45 minutes on a treadmill
in the cold (about 46° F)

00:03:13.320 --> 00:03:16.740
or at closer to room
temperature (about 68° F).

00:03:16.740 --> 00:03:19.380
Participants were then
presented with a buffet meal

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:21.420
in which their eating
was covertly recorded.

00:03:21.420 --> 00:03:24.480
And calorie intake
was significantly greater

00:03:24.480 --> 00:03:25.740
after exercising in the cold.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:29.160
Though walking is often prescribed
for overweight individuals,

00:03:29.160 --> 00:03:32.040
the researchers conclude,
“if walking was to take place

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:36.780
in a cold environment, such as in winter,
then this may stimulate food intake.”

00:03:36.780 --> 00:03:39.960
In the warmer months,
though, obesity researchers

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:42.480
suggest exercising outdoors
may be preferable

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:45.401
to an air-conditioned gym.

00:03:45.600 --> 00:03:49.298
All studies to date on the effects
of hot and cold environments

00:03:49.298 --> 00:03:52.800
have found that exercising
in cool water or under cool conditions

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:56.640
on land led to an increase
in post-workout calorie intake.

00:03:56.640 --> 00:04:00.300
What about a quick dip
in the pool after you exercise?

00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:03.480
Australian researchers found
that immersion in water

00:04:03.480 --> 00:04:06.600
for 15 minutes—cool or warm—
after a running session

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:09.540
resulted in increased
calorie intake.

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:13.020
What is it about getting
wet that whets your appetite?

00:04:13.620 --> 00:04:15.180
Maybe they got
a chill after getting out

00:04:15.180 --> 00:04:17.040
before they could change
into dry clothes?

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.800
This suggests that though
a cool shower after a workout

00:04:19.800 --> 00:04:22.980
may be invigorating, it might
be better to stick to hot.

